    "reading": {
        "body": "Chegg, a public education technology company founded in 2005, has deployed CheggMate, an AI study assistant that raises unresolved questions about content accuracy, student data protection, and academic integrity in its core market.\n\nThe company operates in a sector where AI adoption carries particular friction. CheggMate sits at the intersection of three problems: whether AI-generated homework help meets academic honesty standards at institutions that employ it, whether the training and accuracy of such a system matches the stakes of educational outcomes, and how student data flows through the product. Chegg's existing subscription services already positioned it as a homework and study aid provider. The addition of an AI layer does not change the fundamental tension between helping students learn and enabling them to bypass learning.\n\nThe data does not specify CheggMate's compute requirements, hosting infrastructure, or power footprint. It does not detail how much student data the system ingests, what accuracy benchmarks Chegg has set, or what guardrails it has adopted to address honor code risks. The company's customer survey from late 2024 is noted but its findings are not included here. Without these details, the gap between CheggMate as a product announcement and CheggMate as a deployed system remains opaque.",
